  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nBy Friday, the aforementioned vorticity will begin to sweep\nsoutheast across Iowa with showers likely and a few \nthunderstorms. At 500 mb, the wave axis will be seasonably\nstrong with heights falling to around 582 dam or ~ 2 SD \nanomalies below normal for this time of year. Simultaneously, at\n250 mb a jet streak is forecast to round the base of the \nshortwave trough giving a slight enhancement to the upper level \ndivergence. Regarding instability, the GFS continues to \nadvertise MU cape values approaching 3800 J/kg Friday afternoon.\nTaking a look at the LREF though, MU CAPE values are advertised\nto be much more reasonable around 1600 J/kg. Surface to 6 km \nshear is forecast to be around 35 kt. Given the above, SPC has \noutlooked the region (mainly central Missouri) in a marginal \nrisk. Damaging wind gusts appear to be the primary threat \n(mostly in the afternoon and early evening hours of Friday). \n\nSaturday and beyond is where model guidance starts to diverge.\nIn particular, the deterministic GFS and ECMWF continue to\noscillate back and forth on whether a closed low, or wave\nbreaking, will occur from the Friday vorticity. Why does this\nmatter? If a closed low forms, precipitation chances will likely\nhold on longer (into Sunday and possibly Monday). If a closed\nlow doesn't form (or is really weak and forms east of \nMissouri), the area will likely dry out sooner. Analyzing the \nlatest WPC cluster analysis reveals that the GEFS and GEPS \nensemble suites are most similar and advertise a more \nprogressive solution (not a closed low forming). In fact, the \nEPS which has broad support for a closed low appears under \ndispersive. 50% of the EPS guidance is clustered together with \nonly 15% of the GEPS and 3% of the GEFS. This under dispersion \nof the EPS has allowed us to continue to trend our thinking \ntowards Sunday being more of a dry forecast with below normal \ntemperatures. For the beginning of the next work week, near \nnormal temperatures and mostly dry conditions will persist.",
